[2026-11-16T10:05:54.837Z] [test-st-bf6f22] Thought: Writing the two defect RCA files for the real defects I found (agent tool visibility leak, markdown javascript: href).
[2026-11-16T10:05:54.838Z] [test-st-bf6f22] Action: write_file {"path":".xcoder/defects/defect0001.md","content":"# defect0001 — Agent read tools leak credential, dotfile and generated paths\n\n- **Defect ID:** defect0001\n- **Discovered by:** tester (subtask `test-st-bf6f22`)\n- **Severity:** high\n- **Status:** fixed in this subtask\n\n## Observed behaviour\n\nThe agent's allowlisted read tools enumerated paths that the UI tree is\nrequired to hide. With the seeded demo workspace:\n\n- `list_dir` on the root returned `.git/`, `node_modules/` and `.env` in its\n  summary, e.g. `8 entries in .: .git/, docs/, node_modules/, ...`.\n- `glob` with pattern `**/*` returned `.env` and `.git/config`.\n- `grep` for `SOME_API_KEY` returned a hit of `.env:1`, i.e. the agent could\n  confirm the location and line number of a credential file.\n\n`tree.listChildren` correctly filtered these entries, so the UI hid them while\nthe agent did not.\n\n## Expected behaviour\n\n`README.md` states: \"The agent can only call the 11 allowlisted read tools\",\nand the design (P-02 security notes, S-06) states that dotfiles, generated\ndirectories and credential-shaped names are *never visible*. The allowlist\nrestricts **which tools** may run; the sandbox must restrict **what they can\ntouch**. Hiding a path from the explorer while exposing it to the agent is a\nsecurity boundary with two different answers.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- `.env` must never appear in any tool summary, and its contents must never be\n  readable or greppable (see `crypto-architect` bylaw PRM-09: policy enforced\n  in code, not by the prompt).\n- `node_modules/`, `dist/`, `build/`, `.git/` and `.xcoder/` must not be\n  enumerable by the agent.\n\n## Reproduction steps\n\n1. `npm install` in `xcoder-dojo/`.\n2. Run `npx vitest run --config vitest.config.ts src/backend/__tests__/tools.test.ts`.\n3. Before the fix, three tests failed:\n   - `list_dir > never lists credential-shaped or generated entries`\n   - `glob > does not leak hidden or generated file paths`\n   - `grep > never returns a hit inside a credential file`\n\nEquivalent manual reproduction:\n\n```ts\nconst vfs = createVfs(Date.now());\ndispatchTool(vfs, { toolName: 'grep', args: { pattern: 'SOME_API_KEY' } });\n// -> { outcome: 'ok', summary: '1 match(es) for /SOME_API_KEY/: .env:1' }\n```\n\n## Root cause hypothesis\n\n`dispatchTool` in `src/backend/tools.ts` applies `normalizeRelPath` to the\n*requested* path, which correctly refuses an explicit `.env` argument. But the\nenumeration tools (`list_dir`, `glob`, `grep`) then iterate over\n`vfs.entries` directly and never apply the `isVisibleName` filter that\n`tree.listChildren` applies in `demo-bridge.ts`. The visibility rule lived in\nonly one of the two call sites, so it was a defence with a hole in the other.\n\nFix: add a single `isVisibleRelPath` helper in `tools.ts` that requires every\nsegment to satisfy `isVisibleName`, and apply it to `list_dir` results, `glob`\nmatches and `grep` files. The rule now has one definition and both call sites\nuse it.\n\n## Affected files\n\n- `xcoder-dojo/src/backend/tools.ts` (fixed)\n- `xcoder-dojo/src/backend/__tests__/tools.test.ts` (regression coverage added)\n\n## Verification\n\n`npx vitest run --config vitest.config.ts src/backend/__tests__/tools.test.ts`\n-> 33 tests passed, including the three previously failing leak assertions.\n"}
[2026-11-16T10:05:54.846Z] [test-st-bf6f22] Observation: {"ok":true,"relPath":".xcoder/defects/defect0001.md","bytesWritten":3206}
[2026-11-16T10:06:00.260Z] [test-st-bf6f22] Thought: Writing the second defect RCA for the markdown javascript: href issue.
[2026-11-16T10:06:00.261Z] [test-st-bf6f22] Action: write_file {"path":".xcoder/defects/defect0002.md","content":"# defect0002 — Markdown preview handed `javascript:` URLs to the renderer\n\n- **Defect ID:** defect0002\n- **Discovered by:** tester (subtask `test-st-bf6f22`)\n- **Severity:** high\n- **Status:** fixed in this subtask\n\n## Observed behaviour\n\nThe sanitiser stripped raw HTML and remote images, but a Markdown link with a\nscript scheme survived intact. Parsing\n\n```md\nclick [me](javascript:alert(1))\n```\n\nproduced a link span with `href: 'javascript:alert(1)'`, and `MarkdownView`\nrendered it as `<a href=\"javascript:alert(1)\">me</a>`. Any `data:`,\n`vbscript:` or mixed-case variant behaved the same way.\n\n## Expected behaviour\n\nThe design (T-07, UI-02) says the parser \"never returns HTML\" so the renderer\n\"has nothing dangerous to insert\". A script-scheme href defeats that: the AST\nis data, but the `href` attribute is an executable sink. The UI also renders\nmodel- and workspace-derived text, so this is the classic\n\"raw model output into an interpreter\" path (bylaw PRM-08).\n\nExpected: unsafe schemes are neutralised - the link label survives as inert\ntext and no executable URL reaches the DOM. `http:`, `https:`, `mailto:`, and\nroot-relative/fragment links remain valid, with `http(s)` still flagged\n`external: true` so the renderer can add `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`.\n\n## Reproduction steps\n\n1. `npm install` in `xcoder-dojo/`.\n2. Run `npx vitest run --config vitest.config.ts src/backend/__tests__/markdown.test.ts`.\n3. Before the fix, failed:\n   - `link scheme policy > does not hand a javascript: URL to the renderer`\n     -> `expected the link to be neutralised: expected { kind: 'link', text: 'me' } to be undefined`\n\n## Root cause hypothesis\n\nIn `inline()` (`src/backend/markdown.ts`) the link branch accepted any\nnon-empty destination:\n\n```ts\nconst href = link[2].trim();\nconst external = /^https?:\\/\\//i.test(href);\nspans.push({ kind: 'link', text: link[1], href, external });\n```\n\nThe `external` flag only decides presentation; nothing validated the scheme.\nSanitising was implemented for HTML and images but this third sink was\noverlooked because it looked like \"a link\" rather than \"an href\".\n\nFix: add an allowlist-based `isSafeHref` (`http`, `https`, `mailto`, or no\nscheme at all) and, for anything else, emit the link text as a plain text\nspan. Allowlisting a small set is safer than blocklisting `javascript:`,\nbecause the blocklist always misses a case.\n\n## Affected files\n\n- `xcoder-dojo/src/backend/markdown.ts` (fixed)\n- `xcoder-dojo/src/backend/__tests__/markdown.test.ts` (regression coverage)\n- `xcoder-dojo/src/ui/__tests__/render.test.ts` (render-level regression coverage)\n\n## Verification\n\n`npx vitest run --config vitest.config.ts src/backend/__tests__/markdown.test.ts src/ui/__tests__/render.test.ts`\n-> 38 tests passed, including the new `neutralises a javascript: link` render assertion.\n"}
